   245	It was done in the time that it takes to lace a boot. Cael waited for something to come from the trodden grass, and nothing came. The man with the leather on his wrist had watched the pen make its strokes. He did not nod, by so much as the width of a finger, as he had nodded on a river bank; he stood with his eyes on the paper until it was folded, and then they moved to Cael, and rested there, and told him nothing that he could have written down.
   246	
