   139	She did not often do that. A healer's hands are her eyes, and she carried hers open, as a rule, swinging, where the air could get at them. This evening they felt as they had felt once in her first year on a house's staff, when she had been set to wash a hundred bandages in a trough in the cold and had lost her fingers to the wrist; she could move them, and they would not tell her anything.
   140	
   141	She had put them on that boy three times.
   142	
   143	She had spent three mendings at the table since noon, the whole of a Tide's reserve for a day, and every one had been what it should be. The scald had stood in the arm like water behind a board. The ox-man's foot had been four small bones and a great deal of noise. She had gone into each of them as she went into a house she knew, and found what was stopped, and opened it, and come out tired. That was her trade. She had not once, since the morning her Path woke, laid a hand on a living body and had it come back to her empty.
   144	
   145	Three times, now. Once on a mountain, over three broken ribs, and twice on a scrubbed table.
   146	
   147	She was not a person who guessed. She had been taught by a woman who struck her knuckles for guessing, and she had come to be glad of it. So she went over what her hands had found, as she would have gone over a patient for a second healer, and she kept strictly to that.
   148	
   149	There was bone. Bone answered to anyone; she had felt the breaks as a blind man feels a step. There was movement, past the bone. She was sure of that, and it was what kept her fingers stupid. A dead thing she could have understood. This was more alive than most, and it ran, and she had felt it running; and she could no more have said which way, or between what, than she could have drawn the wind.
   150	
   151	*No banks.* She had told the small pale one so, since the small pale one was writing it down. It was the truest thing she had, and it was not much.
   152	
   153	She tried her hands on the mule, at the first turn of the hill, as a person tries a lamp that has gone out once. She laid her palm on the warm brown neck, under the mane, and counted. It was all there. A beast's bed is broad and shallow and runs slow, and she had felt this one's every day for thirty rounds; it told her of a sound heart, and a near hind hoof that would want the smith inside the month. Her hands were as they had always been.
   154	
   155	That ought to have been a comfort, and it was only half of one. If the fault was not in the instrument, then it lay in the thing the instrument had been put to, and she had no place to keep a thought of that kind.
   156	
   157	The road went up the hill in a long slant, and she went up it below the mule.
   158	
   159	Four people were coming down.
   160	
   161	She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
   162	
   163	Her fingers woke for that. She had not touched it, and she did not need to. It was old, ten years or more; the joint had been out and gone back in by itself, a little wrong, and grown a seat there. She knew what it would cost to unmake that. She knew, too, that the captain had stopped his walk by half a pace to let her see it.
   164	
   165	"You were looked for up the water," he said. "On the eighty-first."
   166	
   167	"I was late."
   168	
   169	He let that be the whole of it. He had a level voice. "Is it a thing you'd do?"
   170	
   171	"Not on a road. Not in a night." She did not soften it; he was not a man one softened things for. "It's two days of me and a week lying down after, and I'm five days behind. Ask me on a round when I'm not."
   172	
   173	He nodded. He had asked, and been told the price, and she saw him put it by. She gave him a nod of her own, as one gives it to a man who has not wasted one's time, and went on up the hill below the mule.
   174	
   175	---
   176	
   177	She was at the next gate on the eighty-sixth, five days after she had said.
   178	
   179	It was the hold that had given her the coat. She did there what she always did. She ate, and slept her two hours, and walked the bench; and at the end of it sat a man whose shoulder had come out of its place a week before, hauling a calf. His brothers had put it back for him, kneeling on his chest, since there was nobody else. It was in. It was not right. She spent two on it that should have been one, and she did not need to be told whose five days those were.
   180	

