   327	The clouded-eyed man clicked his tongue. The cart turned in the yard, with the child waving over the tail at Brom, as children did, and went out by the east gate after the other two, and she heard the bar go across.
   328	
   329	She would not see them again, for they were going two days, or four, to a square with a dot in it, by a road that walked round something.
   330	
   331	That was what the other way cost, and she made herself price it honestly, for it would have been easy to call it a triumph. One got exactly what one was given: she had three sentences, and no means in the world of cross-examining them, and their only witness was on her way east at a mule's pace.
   332	
   333	"Write," she said.
   334	
   335	Her voice shook, which annoyed her.
   336	
   337	"As she said it. All three, with the question mark after the first."
   338	
   339	Cael wrote. She had him read it back, and then a second time, since she could not see the leaf well enough from where she sat, and she corrected nothing.
   340	
   341	"Now underline two words. *Everybody knows.*"
   342	
   343	He ruled a line beneath them with the edge of the notebook's cover.
   344	
   345	"Again."
   346	
   347	"Twice? You told Lira that a heading hadn't earned two."
   348	
   349	"This has. Mind where you put them: they don't go under *quiet ground*, which is her noun, and I can't source it; I don't know what it is. But she said that everybody knows it, and that's a claim about a whole country, and I have just watched it be true of the first person I didn't ask."
   350	
   351	He ruled the second line, and he was looking at her, she saw, with the careful face he wore for a thing he meant to remember.
   352	
   353	"Is it a source?" he said.
   354	
   355	"Not a good one. The first."
   356	
   357	She had him fold the leaf, and carry it into the barn, and lay it in the middle box, with the corner that was worn white. Then she sat on the tail-board with her hands in her lap, looking at the east gate.
   358	
   359	Seln had gone out by that gate on the forty-fifth day, at noon, and walked two hours along the road; she knew, for she had seen the dust on him. The hide said two days. She did the subtraction, and kept the answer, and put no question to him either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:"It's that. The door's shut, and I'm on the wrong side of it from where I keep the thing." He let his arms fall. "It's queer. I've not used it since the ford, and I'd have told you I'd got used to going without. But I always knew where it was. I could feel it in the next room, as you can feel a fire through a wall. There's no next room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:207:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:223:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:197:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:223:Pike took it without a word. He read it twice, though he could have said it in his sleep, and then he turned his shoulder to the meadow and gave it out: the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the rise, and the hundred. It was the first time in a month that those words had been heard on that grass. He said the whole of it a second time. Then he said *taken*, and five names, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:She had been taught to do it at fourteen, by attending to a place behind the breastbone, and it had been years since she had needed to do it on purpose; it was simply there when she wanted it, as a clerk is there behind a counter. It came now. It hung at the edge of her sight where it had always hung, small and steady and yellow-white, and it had about it the particular quality of attention that she had never been able to describe to anybody who did not have one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:95:"That is where the next mark would fall if the line ran straight," she said. "It won't. Watch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:"That's the Fallow," said Oryn, and clicked her tongue to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had halted on a level of short turf where a spring came out. Brom had been helped off with the box and lay on his back beside it with his arms spread. The others had gone to the water. Lira had not; she had sat down next to Cael, and stretched out both legs, and was looking at Hesk's book where it lay on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:The Fallow road ran east for the rest of that day, and it was the emptiest country that he had ever walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:"The holds either side won't put a beast on it," said Oryn. "I've asked. They don't give you a reason. They say it's fallow, as you'd say a field was resting, and then they talk about something else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:235:*Ninety-fifth day. The Fallow road, at the rise. Eighty strides east of where the ruts turn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not make a sentence of it. He gave it as he would have given Pike a line: a paper off the plank, under a broker's seal; something to be fetched from a place on the Fallow road, two days east, at a rise; out by the east gate at first light. He said what the place was called, and what Pike called it, and he asked her for nothing. He had no more right to that sentence than he had had at a well, and he found that it was easier to leave unsaid the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:She took up the cheek-strap, and let it fall again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:131:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:"That's the Fallow Ring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:169:"I've walked past the Fallow Ring two hundred times. Never once stepped over the line. Thirty years on the roads and I've never met a practitioner who did it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:225:*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:3:Karis made her long cord along Thornwater's fence in the first grey light, with Brom to do the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:201:"My Arbiter's gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:"Smallest first," she said. She had ruled a fresh leaf into five rows and given it to Cael, with the pencil. "A one-armed man has told us that nothing can be declared in there, and five of us felt our Arbiters go, and I believe both. I believed in a ledge as well. There are prints in the grass on the far side of this field, and I am not going to learn that he was mistaken by having Lira crack the sky open a quarter of a mile from whatever left them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:For forty-three days he had refused that Path, and Cael had watched him do it on a ferry, and in a wash, and under a cart on a crust of rock. He had seen the grey start at those wrists whenever a load came on suddenly, and each time he had seen Brom put it down again, like a man pressing a lid onto a pot; it had been the hardest kind of work, and it had been done every hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:There was the grass, and the short noon shadows, and a stride beyond the stone a patch of bent stems where two boots had been. Lira made a small sound. She had the best eyes of the five, and Cael saw them go the whole length of the slope and come back with nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:He was crouched low against a tussock of the pale grass, side on to them, with his face turned down into the crook of his arm and his hands drawn up into his sleeves. He had put himself to the south of them, where the low sun came from behind him into their eyes. There was no Path in it, and no trick that a child could not have understood once it was shown; and it had still taken five people, all of them looking for him, the better part of a breath to find a man in a dark coat on an open hillside at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:113:"That isn't a trial of anything," said Karis. But she was looking at the bent grass, and at where he had got to, and her pencil hand had twitched. "I suppose that it is a trial of you. Third row. *Path sought; none. Practitioner not found for a count of two.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:165:[[TIER-B: Lira, of her Arbiter's return on stepping back over the line — "like a clock that stopped and started again without losing the time." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:207:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:257:He had been certain, in the part of himself that kept accounts, of what would happen: a thing taken out of a Path would stop where Paths stopped. He had supposed it since a plank, as the others supposed it, and for the same reason, which was that no other sum came out even; and he had braced for the read to go from him as a count had gone from a healer's lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:She counted them, and at each crossing she made herself attend to one thing only, as she would have made a student attend. The third time she watched for the instant, and it had no width that she could find. The fifth time she went over slowly, a hand's breadth at a time, to learn whether it dimmed; it did not, and then it was gone. The seventh time she tried to hold it. She fixed the whole of her will on that small bright mark and carried it forward as one carries a full cup, and it went out between one heartbeat and the next without the least sense that anything had been taken from her. That was the part she liked least. She had not been robbed. She had merely looked down and found her hands empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:43:It was very tidy, and she made herself look at that, because it was what she was afraid of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:79:"You keep the stone. Somebody must be standing at the place where we began, so that we know it when we come round to it from the other side. And I should like one of us to be outside the whole time, with the mule, where an Arbiter is an Arbiter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:103:He had put down sixty-three lines at her dictation on a mountain, and this was his third on a hillside, and it was of another kind from any of those. On the bench he had written what a cord and a clock could show to anybody who came after. Here the only instruments were two people's Arbiters, going out and coming back. He had none to offer, and so he held the pencil for the people who had; and he did not grudge it, which surprised him a little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:That made three. He could rule the line now, and it would be straight. What he could not do was say which way along it he was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:161:They went round the north side with the ruts beside them. He could see where the carts had gone, forty yards off, keeping the same distance from the line the whole way, as exactly as if the carters had had a cord of their own; and he supposed that in a sense they had. A man who walked in front of a mule here for twenty-two years, with a Path in him, would have learned to the yard where he ceased to be comfortable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:187:From the south he had the whole of it in one view, for the first and only time. The grey stone was away on his left hand, with a small brown mule beside it. Beyond it the ruts swung out round the north in their wide curve. The floor lay in the middle of everything, with its eleven broken stones, and on the far side, where no turf had been cut, there was the low place in the grass where Seln had knelt. Nothing moved in the whole of it but themselves. It looked like the plainest piece of country in the world, and five people had just walked the edge of it with their Arbiters going out and coming back at every stumble.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:213:"A circle is a made shape."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It lay as it had lain on four other crossings, dry and red, thirty yards from lip to lip, with a ramp cut down either side and the thorn stump standing on its eastern edge. The carter took his mules through at a walk and halted them on the far flat to blow, and the five sat down among the chock-stones where they had twice made a camp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:247:Karis got down, and crossed the yard with her hands held a little away from her sides, as she had carried them when they were bound, and stood before the lamp. Cael saw her draw a breath. Then her right hand made the smallest dip that he had ever seen it make, as a person sets a full stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:She said it quite quietly. Two lines that had stood over theirs since the day they came now stood under them, and the shingle pair were at the foot still. Cael looked for what the rung was made of, since he had learned in this room that it was always made of something. It was a mountain, less a cart. The wall had no place to say so. It said only that a hold which asked Pike who ought to walk a bad hill would now be given five names before it was given two others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:111:Cael had set the cream sheet in the middle of the table, where all five could reach it, and he gave the order as he would have given it for any contract: each of them to speak, and nobody to answer until the last had done. Brom poured. He had allowed one jug, on the strength of a line said at a plank, and had made it plain that the paper under the lamp had not yet earned a second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:121:"He says that there is a place on a road where an Arbiter stops. He says it stops at a line, and that a person knows the line by her own. If that is so, then it can be found with nothing but feet, by anybody, and found again in the same place the next day by somebody else. I have been taught that an Arbiter cannot be tested at all; that it is the one thing every instrument we own takes for granted. I should like to stand where one fails." She drew a breath. "I would go for nothing. I say so in order that nobody should count my hand as a vote for the hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:145:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:227:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five. The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:51:The five waded after. It was a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of quick water, cold enough to make the bones of his feet ache. The tape told him where the edge was whenever a stone turned under him. He kept his breath shallow and his eyes on the cart's tail. Three boxes sat on it under a sheet of patched canvas, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward. On top of them, roped down by her own instructions, sat Karis, who had been lifted up there like a sack and had made no objection.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:The first prints were a man's, of the middle size, set down evenly. Beside the right foot, here and there, was a small round dent, where the haft of a hand-axe had swung at his thigh and touched ground on the slope. The second walker had grounded a spear at every step. The third set of marks was shorter on the left side than on the right by the width of two fingers, every stride, and no deeper for it; whoever made them had been favouring that hip for so long that the body no longer argued. The fourth were the smallest feet of the four and went in the deepest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:67:He had not expected to enjoy it. But there was a pleasure in reading a thing well made, and he had it now, as he had had it from the east tier of a yard, watching a good pair work. The difference was that he did not want to beat this one. He had no notion what he wanted from it; the man had told him to be good enough to be glad of, and Cael was reading the silt to learn what *good enough* walked like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:91:Brom made a sound into his sleeve. Lira looked at the boots, and at the road going away south between its alders, six days of it, and at her own bare foot with the linen crossed over it in a figure of eight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:161:Cael had half risen to say something, and he sat down again. He had been on the point of telling Brom that the week was out, and that he was free of it. He saw now that it would have been like telling a man the price of something he had made himself. Nobody had kept that week but Brom. He could finish it in whatever way he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:167:The valley had flattened and gone wet. For two hundred yards and more in front of them there was no pasture and no alder, only black water standing in sheets among yellow reed, with tussocks between as big as kneeling sheep; and across the whole of it somebody had laid a road. It was made of logs. They lay side by side, crosswise, each as thick as a man's leg and twice as long as the cart was wide, bedded on brushwood and sunk to half their depth. They ran away over the moss in a grey ribbed strip, like the backbone of something very long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:185:It was the fan again, Cael thought, and he was glad to see that it was the fan made small; there was no slope in it and nothing overhead. But somebody had to learn which logs were sound before a wheel was trusted to any of them, and there was only one way that the five of them had ever done that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:207:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:223:They made their last camp on the evening of the eighty-ninth day, on a halting-ground at the top of a long hill, with a river shining below them in the dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:157:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:175:"You'll know," he said. "Your Arbiter tells you. It stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:He had not meant to say it aloud. It had been going round in him since the plank, a thing that he could not sort. A man had walked a road for thirty years, and each time that he came to a certain place he had gone round, and each time he had made a mark in his head. He had never once put his foot over, and he knew the number of his own refusals to the unit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:It was true, and he had not known it, and she had. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for ninety days, and thought himself a careful person; but a refusal that is made again at every stride is the opposite of a rule, for it is a door that somebody is holding shut with his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:271:"So do I." She said it low, and she was still looking at the road. "He said your Arbiter tells you. It stops. I've spent three months not doing the one thing I'm good at, on purpose, a stride at a time, and it went off anyway when a ledge fell. I'd like to stand somewhere that I couldn't. I'd like to know what's left when it isn't me doing the *not*."

