editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:13:So he walked back down the grass with two letters, and thirty people watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It lay as it had lain on four other crossings, dry and red, thirty yards from lip to lip, with a ramp cut down either side and the thorn stump standing on its eastern edge. The carter took his mules through at a walk and halted them on the far flat to blow, and the five sat down among the chock-stones where they had twice made a camp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:"Bracken. A map reference to the Line and nothing past it. Somebody up a stair wanted to know which way we'd gone, and he gave them a place that everybody already knew we'd been, and wrote it out properly, I expect, with a number." She was grinning. "I never liked him. I'd like to shake his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:"Two days out. Two back. Whatever you spend there. That has you at this gate again by the ninety-ninth, if nothing goes wrong, and I've walked behind you people long enough to know what *if* is worth." She held up a thick finger. "That's one reason. I want a patient whole and still for the long reading, and I've seen what your crew brings home from a road. I'll not have him back here on the ninety-ninth with something new broken, and wait three weeks more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:207:"Six gates wait on me the same days, whether I spend them at this table or behind you on that road. I know who's at every one. It's this bench that goes short, and I'll pay it when I come back through." Then she looked straight at Cael, as she had looked at him once across a hollow of shale. "You're a stop. I've added you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:251:It was no bigger than a grain of barley; it caught, and the flame came up round it, ordinary and smoking, and the seed was gone into it, and Karis let out her breath in something that was not quite a laugh. He had last seen her do that across sixty yards of mountain, with her hands shaking like a rope under load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:3:The ferryman would not take silver for his rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"Sixty yards of four-strand. I laid that rope up myself, in the cold term, with him turning the hook." He jerked his chin at his boy. Then he looked at Karis, who had the purse out on her knee, and his long face soured further. "Put that away. What'd I do with it? There's not a rope-walk nearer than the turf fire, and I can't draw a wagon out of a river with marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:"A rope. When you've one. I'll not put a figure on it, for a figure's a thing you can pay and walk off from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:Karis wrote it down as he had said it. *Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman. No price.* She put the purse away uncounted, and Cael thought that it was the first debt she had ever entered without a number beside it, and that she did not like the look of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:39:*HOLD.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:73:Pike nodded slowly. He opened the book at the leaf with five names, and set the flat stone on its upper corner, and turned his shoulder to the meadow. There were perhaps thirty people within hearing, and Cael saw the hide-woman among them, with her knife on her thigh, and under the ash an old carter who lifted his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:105:He turned that over, since he had expected to. On the bank at Oxhollow he had written that he kept waiting to mind, and he had gone on waiting for six days of road. The line said that they had lost a cart, and it said so to thirty strangers, twice. And he stood under an eave with five bands of linen round his chest and felt only that a thing had been counted, by a person who counted everything alike; it was the seventh line under their names, and it was worth sixty-four, and both of those were so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:109:"I want one thing straight, for my own page," she said. "The seventy is in here. I counted it at a ford, and it hasn't altered on the road. Earned stands at a hundred and thirty-two. What you have written under that line is another figure, and I don't believe that it is money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:151:He did not need to look, for he knew whose it was, and he knew what was folded in the middle box, forty yards off, with two lines ruled under two of its words. Karis had heard those two words once before, dropped by a woman with a sack of barley on her hip, and she had asked nobody what they meant for thirty-two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:201:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:He said that last quite mildly, and Cael understood that he had been told something about the man. Pike wrote down whatever was put in front of him and read it to anyone; but he had found, in thirty years, one paper that he would not hang up between two fences and a wheel, and he had kept it where a person must go looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:241:"A hundred and eight," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:245:The account was square, as far as coin could square it. There remained a rope with no figure against it, and a line in a book that said *cart lost*, and neither of those could be paid off; they could only be added to, by whatever the five of them did next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:He had not meant to say it aloud. It had been going round in him since the plank, a thing that he could not sort. A man had walked a road for thirty years, and each time that he came to a certain place he had gone round, and each time he had made a mark in his head. He had never once put his foot over, and he knew the number of his own refusals to the unit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:61:*HOLD.* It was in the square capitals, and it was not his. There were five names under it, and five again under the same word on the shingle beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred. That was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:111:Cael had set the cream sheet in the middle of the table, where all five could reach it, and he gave the order as he would have given it for any contract: each of them to speak, and nobody to answer until the last had done. Brom poured. He had allowed one jug, on the strength of a line said at a plank, and had made it plain that the paper under the lamp had not yet earned a second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:131:He pushed the paper back to the middle of the table and picked up his cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:145:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:179:For some time the only sound was the fire. Cael looked at a cream sheet that had lain a month under a river stone, and saw it as he had not seen it at the plank: a thing with a ruled margin, lying on an inn's boards in a country that kept no paper. He had walked east for ninety days, and here was a piece of the west on his own table, with a seal on it that told him nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:197:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:213:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:227:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five. The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:241:"So we could go four days and come home with a line and no money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:Two of the three boxes were to stay in the loft, and so two of the three box-ropes were free. Brom married them end to end with a splice that he worked by feel, and hung the whole length from a fence-post with a stone on its end to take the stretch out, while she stood by with her short cord, the one that was knotted at every foot. When the rope had done creaking she laid the short cord along it again and again, and at each length she tied in a scrap of red wool from the hold's mending basket. Cael watched her count. She told nobody the figure, and wrote it in her notebook with her shoulder turned, as a person writes down the weight of his own purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:7:"It's a cord now," she said, coiling it. "It was two ropes, and they are not to be thought of as ropes again until I have done with them. Nobody is to tie a box with this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:141:Karis had her notebook open, and her pencil was not moving. Cael knew what that stillness cost her. A primary source was sitting across a fire from her, pulling on a boot, and had been round the place thirty times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:179:"Then we shall have walked to a ring of stones, and a one-armed man has been wrong for thirty years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:253:"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:5:Cael stayed where he was, on the gravel at the water's edge, with the book shut on his knee and his left arm held in against five bands of linen. It was the start of something, and so he took the inventory, as he took it at the start of anything. Three ribs were broken on the left side, low; they had been taped on the eighty-first day, and the tape was to come off on the ninety-ninth and not before. The calf was bound in a healer's cloth and was closing by itself, as his hurts did. Lira had a shin that was four days old, and an ankle that no healer on this road was going to mend, and one boot. Brom's left wrist wore a linen cuff, and he was to hang nothing from it until the middle of the eighty-seventh day. Karis had been told to ask nothing of herself for three days more, and she was asleep against a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:7:There were three boxes, and they stood on the bare ground by a gate-house wall. That was the item he liked least. A hundred and sixteen marks lay in Karis's purse, and six of them were a smith's. The plank where those six would be paid was six days down a road, and each box weighed as much as a grown sheep. He had one man who was allowed to carry, and four who were not fit to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:33:He asked them in the yard, by the well, with the light gone grey. Brom looked at three boxes and then at his own left wrist, and Cael watched him weigh a road of six days against a rule of three, and put his hand up. Lira's was up already. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. They had to wake Karis for hers, and she raised it before her eyes were properly open and asked afterward what she had voted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:51:The five waded after. It was a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of quick water, cold enough to make the bones of his feet ache. The tape told him where the edge was whenever a stone turned under him. He kept his breath shallow and his eyes on the cart's tail. Three boxes sat on it under a sheet of patched canvas, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward. On top of them, roped down by her own instructions, sat Karis, who had been lifted up there like a sack and had made no objection.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:95:"I heard." The purse came down over the cart's side in a small pale hand. "I shall want it understood that this is the board's money and it has bought a boot and a half's worth of leather that nobody can wear. In hand, a hundred and fourteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:203:Brom looked at them. He had stood so in front of a cart in a wash once, Cael remembered, and in front of two hundred and sixty yards of scree, working out a weight. He was not working anything out now. He walked up to the middle box, with the corner that was worn white, and bent his knees, and took hold of it by its two rope beckets, one in each hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:207:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:"Tomorrow is the ninetieth," she said. "The plank, and a smith. I have a slip in my coat with a horned head on it, and I want to hear what he does with a job that ended in a no." She turned a leaf. "And Thornwater on the ninety-third. That's four days, and two of them are road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:257:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:165:[[TIER-B: Lira, of her Arbiter's return on stepping back over the line — "like a clock that stopped and started again without losing the time." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:[[TIER-B: Pike, in the board's room, of Teague — "Teague's nine up… He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice… I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:129:[[TIER-B: Brom, at the council — "A survey kit's a cart's worth… That's not the reason. That's what it is." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:3:That same night, in the second watch, Karis went back to the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:There could be no lamp. She had lit one in a yard two evenings ago and been absurdly glad of it, and she would not have set a point here for all the paper in Ternhall. She knelt down in the frozen grass on the western side of the stone and laid the notebook open on her knee. The stars were out. She could see the white of the page and not much else, and she resolved to write large, one line to a leaf, and sort it by daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:She had been empty for nine days. She had spent every point she owned on a mountain in eight seconds, and afterward she had not been able to strike a flint. But in all those nine days the sigil had been there. She had called it on the first night under the pale rock, and it had come, faithful as ever, and when she asked it for a point it had simply had nothing to give her. That had been a counter with a clerk behind it and an empty till.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:35:It was the only measurement that she could take tonight, and she took it with the stubbornness of a person who has been given one tool. From the turn of the ruts to the stone it was eighty of Cael's strides, by his count, and his were a yard. She paced it herself, there and back, and her own count was a good deal larger, as it always was. She wrote both. She paced it a second time and was one stride out, and wrote that too, and the mean of the pair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:39:A hand held out across the stone did nothing at all; the sigil hung where it was. She had watched the healer stand so at dusk, for a long while, and had not asked her what she felt. An arm did nothing. But when she stood astride the stone itself, with one boot on either side, she had the sigil or had it not according to how she leaned, and Karis would not write down a lean. She put: *Narrower than one stride of mine. No finer figure tonight. Wants daylight, and a second person to watch my feet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:49:Her pencil had come down on the page. She looked at the point of it, resting on white paper in the starlight, and she knew exactly what kind of sentence it was about to begin. It would have had *as though* in it. It would have said what the thing was like; and a likeness, set down in a notebook at midnight, is a hypothesis that has not yet had the honesty to call itself one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:53:*Five rows,* she wrote instead, and ruled them by touch. *K. L. B. S. O. Each: gone at the stone, going east. Back at the stone, coming west. One stride. Observed by all five at once on the 95th; by me alone, nine times, that night.* And under that, because she kept that sort of account as well: *I am frightened by how well it agrees. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:125:"That's the registry's stamp," said Lira. "That's theirs. What's it doing in a field two days past a fence that isn't on their map?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:133:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:141:"I am finishing a count. If I stop at every wonder on this hillside we shall be here at dusk with half a shape, and I had rather have a whole one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:143:They went on. Cael walked the fourth cord with his head turned back over his shoulder. The bar stood in the grass where they had left it, brown and small, beside a square of black turf; and he thought that it was a strange thing to have crossed a Line, and a mountain, and ninety-six days of road, and to be met at the far end of all of it by two numerals that somebody at a counter had signed for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:163:From the north the ring looked different. The eleven uprights had closed up and overlapped, and between two of the tallest he had his first clear sight of what lay among them. It was a floor, grey and pale and perfectly level, raised a little above the grass like the top of a low table; and the morning light lay on it without a shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:173:"Both. That one is this morning's, and it points inward. This is older, and it goes out. They use these as a hold uses its gate." Seln had not risen. "I cannot give you a number from a path. I can say that it is not one animal, or two. And I can say that I have gone the whole of the north side and not found a single run. They come and go by the east."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:181:"We go in at noon," he said. "Not before; it's the hour they've never once been seen abroad, on a stretch or at a ford. And we're out again and on the west side of that stone with the sun two hands above the hills. Whatever isn't done by then isn't done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:231:"I do not know what the number counts, and I shall not guess." Seln rose. He stood with his hands behind him and looked from the stake to the ring, and back along the line of turfs to the stone, as he would have looked over a room that somebody had left in a hurry. "It stands a boot's width from where she stopped. She found that place with her feet, this morning, knowing nothing. Whoever drove this found the same place to the same boot's width."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:43:He read the second letter at noon, on the far side of the hills, since it was a noon with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:He had thought about that on the forty-ninth night, and it came back to him now on a turf bank two days past a fence. There was a woman in Ardenmere who kept a straight ledger of a fighting yard, and a one-armed man in a meadow who kept a straight book of a plank; and neither had the least idea that the other was alive. He was written in both. He did not think that any registry on the continent could have said as much of one of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:The Fallow road ran east for the rest of that day, and it was the emptiest country that he had ever walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:119:He watched the healer walk, since it had become a habit. She kept the carter's pace. She was below the mule on every slope, though the slopes were so gentle here that he could not always have said which side was the low one, and she changed over without seeming to think. He set himself to her beat, and breathed to the tape, and for long stretches of that afternoon the tape had nothing to say to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:123:"One more night of that," said Seln, feeding it. "I should not light one tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:135:"That's there," she said. "It's sound, and it's yours, and I'd know it in the dark by now. I'll want it again the day after tomorrow, at this ring, so don't do anything to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:153:"The day after tomorrow," she said, half to herself, "there will be six of us who saw it. I mean to write down who."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:157:They came to the rise late on the ninety-fifth day, with the sun low at their backs and their shadows going on ahead of them up the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:161:The road turned. He had walked it for two days as straight as a plumb-line, and here, at the crest, both ruts swung out to the left together, toward the north, in a wide easy curve, like a stream going round a rock; and a long way off, beyond the curve's far end, he could see them come back and go on eastward along the line that they had left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:221:The nine fragments lay in him where they had lain since a ford. He was sure of that as well. When Lira's hand went up, Wind had leaned in his own feet to go with hers, as it had leaned every day for two years, and he had put it down without thinking; and that had been on the far side of where the stone now stood. Anchor was asking about the stone already. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:235:*Ninety-fifth day. The Fallow road, at the rise. Eighty strides east of where the ruts turn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:255:Shadow lifted then, as it lifted at every dusk. Cael felt it come up soft behind the other eight, as it had come up beside a turf fire on the fifth day and at a ford on the fifty-third, offering to take him out of the light; and he told it no. It had come at the turn of the ruts as it came anywhere. He wondered whether it would have come a stride beyond the stone, and thought that he knew, and did not go to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:257:He walked up to the crest once more before he lay down, since he wished to see the place with the day going off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:3:The ferry brought its sack at noon, and Pike gave out the mail as he gave out everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:He had sent one letter west to her, on the fourth day, by a courier with a split lip, and it had carried no place to answer to; to the other he had sent none at all. Both of them had written into the air, as an old man in Denvash had, and the air had brought both to the one plank that there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:31:"He's quite right," said Brom, with feeling. "I cut a loaf and a half into fifteen on a mountain, and it was the only thing that week that came out even. A letter's no different. You don't eat it all the day it comes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:He put them both inside Hesk's book, between the leaves, where a cream sheet had lain the night before. It was a small thing to have decided; but he had gone a long time with nothing in front of him except what he was holding down, and he liked the weight of two unbroken seals a good deal more than he would have liked knowing what was under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:43:They were at the wash by noon of the ninety-second day, with the cart behind them and the saddle behind the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:63:*The Crown yard has a new cohort captain. The intake came up the bluff a fortnight ago, forty-one of them, and they've put a third-year over them who was a second-year when you left and whose name you would not know, so I'll not spend the ink. He is fair. He counts slower than you did. They stand where he puts them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:Cael folded the sheet along its creases. He had two things now, and he laid them side by side in his head, as Karis had once laid a noun beside six numbers. Somebody above a registry had asked which way certain people had gone; and a paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, written by a hand that had learned its trade at a ruled desk, sending whoever took it two days east of a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:109:Karis had the printed sheet out that evening, for the first time since a shelf above a valley with no name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:113:"Come and look," she said. "I want it seen by more eyes than mine. I have carried this for ninety-two days and not opened it once, since I knew what was on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:133:It looked a poor thing beside the print. It was scraped calfskin, brown at the edges, drawn in oak-gall and burnt stick by a man who could not write his name; and it had on it every ford between the river and the eastern holds, and every hold as a square with a dot, and the days as ticks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:137:She showed them. The first they knew: it was the double line that left its straight, two days beyond Thornwater, and went round a patch of bare skin and came back, and she had ringed the patch in pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:"That is the only place where one of his roads walks round a blank. I said so on the fifty-eighth day, and it is still so. But he draws a hill with short hatching, and water with three wavy strokes. Look here, south of the ring with the bar, a day down and a little aside. He has hatched hills all across this corner, close as thatch, and in the middle of the hatching there is a round place the size of my thumbnail where he lifted the stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:143:"And here, a long way south-east, among his water. Strokes all round, and a round of bare skin in the midst, with no road within a day of it." She had ringed both. "Three blank circles. One I can source, since a one-armed man has named it. The other two are a man lifting his hand from a skin. I have written *guess* beside each, and I shall not call them anything else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:147:"On this? No. It is a map of days. He has drawn a day's haul the same length uphill and down, and bent his roads to fit the skin. I could lay an edge across it and learn something about the calf." She settled back on her heels, and her eyes went from the one to the other, the black print and the brown hide, lying side by side on a carters' halting-ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:155:They came up to Thornwater's gate an hour after noon on the ninety-third day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:157:The ford had been only a ford. Its white stones ran out along the lip of the sill to the bar, a yard apart, without a gap, and there was nothing on the gravel but a wagtail; he had looked down the bar to its tail all the same, and at the bend below, where two had gone on the fifty-third evening and not been seen since. They took the stretch at noon, a mile of pale grass with the frost still lying in its roots and a white stone standing in it, and he walked it with his hands at his sides and nine things shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:167:Her eyes were shut and her lips were still, and the sweat had come out along her cropped hair in the cold; and Cael understood that she had come through that gate herself not long before them, and had sat down to this without so much as slipping a pannier. She had owed it for fifteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:175:"Fifteen days. Since the break."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:183:"Ninth day. I said nine. You've counted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:187:"I count days. It's the only thing I do count." She got up off the plank by pushing on her knees. "I eat now, and I'm on my back two hours, and then there's a yard of people. You can tell me where you're going while I've a bowl in my hand. Five don't walk up this valley in the cold to keep an appointment about three ribs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not make a sentence of it. He gave it as he would have given Pike a line: a paper off the plank, under a broker's seal; something to be fetched from a place on the Fallow road, two days east, at a rise; out by the east gate at first light. He said what the place was called, and what Pike called it, and he asked her for nothing. He had no more right to that sentence than he had had at a well, and he found that it was easier to leave unsaid the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:"Two days out. Two back. Whatever you spend there. That has you at this gate again by the ninety-ninth, if nothing goes wrong, and I've walked behind you people long enough to know what *if* is worth." She held up a thick finger. "That's one reason. I want a patient whole and still for the long reading, and I've seen what your crew brings home from a road. I'll not have him back here on the ninety-ninth with something new broken, and wait three weeks more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:207:"Six gates wait on me the same days, whether I spend them at this table or behind you on that road. I know who's at every one. It's this bench that goes short, and I'll pay it when I come back through." Then she looked straight at Cael, as she had looked at him once across a hollow of shale. "You're a stop. I've added you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:He stood at the long house door with an empty bowl on the sill beside him. She had a round of seven gates, and she was late at every one of them already, and nobody had asked this of her; and she had just spent five days of it on a road that every carter she had ever mended told her to keep off. He had wanted to say the sentence for nine days. He was glad now, with his whole heart, that he had left it for her, since from him it would have been a request, and from her it was a decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:219:From the loft door, that evening, with his legs hanging over the yard, he watched the healer work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:227:There was a bench under its window, and by dusk there were seven people on it, three of the hold and the rest come in with the east carts, and one of them a child. Oryn came out when the shadow of the fence had crossed the well, and washed to the elbow, and went along them as he had seen her go along eight at Oxhollow; and she chose two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:237:"I told you a week, on a mountain, and three days on top of it at a ford. That ran out on the eighty-seventh, and this is the ninety-third. Have you asked it for anything since?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:243:"Because I have been on roads, where one does not. And because—" Karis stopped. She did not often stop in the middle of a sentence. "Because I looked for the place, the first night, and it was gone. I had sooner not look again and find it gone a second time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:3:They held the trials at the stone, at noon, and Karis set the order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:15:The sun stood as high as it would go at that time of year, pale and small, and their shadows lay short on the frosted grass. They had gathered on the western side of the grey stone, with the turn of the ruts at their backs and the ring two hundred yards and more in front. Oryn had the mule by the cheek-strap a few yards behind the rest, and her free hand tucked into her armpit for warmth; she had watched the whole of the morning from that stone without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:He wrote it, and looked at it a breath longer than a line needed. He had sat in a whitewashed room for the better part of a year while that small exact person laid points on a wall for him to study, and he carried one of them still, the fifth of his nine, with its rent paid; and he had not known until this noon that there was a place anywhere where her hand could make that dip and have nothing come of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:For forty-three days he had refused that Path, and Cael had watched him do it on a ferry, and in a wash, and under a cart on a crust of rock. He had seen the grey start at those wrists whenever a load came on suddenly, and each time he had seen Brom put it down again, like a man pressing a lid onto a pot; it had been the hardest kind of work, and it had been done every hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:71:"What's it like?" said Lira. "You said a door, yesterday. Closing behind you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:93:He stepped across, and for a breath he only stood, with his eyes half shut. Cael knew what he was reaching for, since he carried a piece of the same thing himself, sealed, and had felt it lift in him at every dusk for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:There was the grass, and the short noon shadows, and a stride beyond the stone a patch of bent stems where two boots had been. Lira made a small sound. She had the best eyes of the five, and Cael saw them go the whole length of the slope and come back with nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:He was crouched low against a tussock of the pale grass, side on to them, with his face turned down into the crook of his arm and his hands drawn up into his sleeves. He had put himself to the south of them, where the low sun came from behind him into their eyes. There was no Path in it, and no trick that a child could not have understood once it was shown; and it had still taken five people, all of them looking for him, the better part of a breath to find a man in a dark coat on an open hillside at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:121:She sat down on the grass outside the line and took off the great boot, and unwound the whole of a spare shirt, and stood up on her own taped foot with the frost under it. She was to run on nothing for a fortnight yet, and nobody reminded her of it; this was one stride, and she had plainly weighed it against a healer's month before she touched the lace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:"I know what it'll be. I heard it come back off a mountain." Lira's face was pale. "It went off by itself, that morning. I never told it to. I've got to walk into a place with dogs in it on this foot, and I'd like to know, before I do, whether it can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:He had watched her do that a thousand times, from a rail at Norhold, and on a gravel bar in the mist, and at the mouth of a ledge on the seventy-eighth morning. He knew the order of it as he knew his own name: the weight going forward onto the balls of both feet, and the heels lifting, and then the gathering in her calves that came a breath before the air broke. For three months he had seen her reach that last place and refuse it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:159:"Back. All at once." She had her hand flat against her own breastbone. "I felt it at the stone last night, but I wasn't looking then. I was looking this time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:193:"Nobody has that I ever met. That's why I walked two days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:233:Cael looked at her as she said it. She had walked two days up an empty road to learn that the one thing she owned in the world, besides a route, would be taken out of her hands at a stone; and having learned it she was going in regardless, with her sleeves pushed back, to be of whatever use a pair of ordinary hands could be. He had not asked her to come. He did not think that he would have had the face to ask it, knowing what a stride had just cost her, and he saw that she would have come all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:297:It was the first thing of that kind that he had kept from any of them in a long while, and he had done it at a stone, at noon, with a pencil going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:3:Six of them went over the stone a little after noon, and the mule stayed where she was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:7:They took very little. Brom had the middle box on his back, with the chips in it and the paper, since whatever a surveyor had left would want carrying home in something. Karis had the long cord and the short one. There was the hedging bill, and Lira's knife, and Seln's, and a skin of water. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:51:"The same gap from one side to the other, to the thickness of a knot. And no pitch. I have laid this brass on a mountain for seven days, and on every hundred of it the thread hung to one side by so many hands. Here it hangs on its mark whichever way I point it." She sat back on her heels. "A threshing floor is not as flat as this. A table is not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:It took the rest of the afternoon. She would not copy by eye, since she said that an eye puts in what it expects to see; she laid a clean sheet against the band, and had Lira hold it flat with both hands, and rubbed the side of her pencil over it until the lines came up white out of the grey. She took a sheet to a stone. She numbered each in its corner, and wrote beside the number how high the stone stood, and which way it faced, and Brom carried them to the box, one at a time, on the flat of his two hands, as he would have carried a tray of eggs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:121:"Then it stays shut," said Brom. "We've walked four days for a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:127:It had come round into the west while they worked, and it stood now above the hills that they had crossed two days ago. He put his arm out straight and laid his hand along the skyline, fingers together, and then his other hand on top of that. The lower edge of the sun sat on his upper knuckle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:129:Two hands. He had said it at the eastern runs that morning, with a print in front of him, and Seln had said that he would hold him to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:133:He weighed it. He had set a limit, and he knew what a limit was worth that gave by a quarter of an hour the first time it was leaned on. He also knew that nine sheets out of eleven was a thing she would carry for the rest of her life. And the hillside was as empty as it had been at noon. No bird had got up. Nothing had stirred the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:143:Oryn had sat down on the western lip with her boot off. She had watched the whole afternoon from there, pressing the strip on her heel now and then with her thumb, and she had not once asked what any of it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:173:He looked at it a piece at a time, as he looked at everything. There were prints. They lay one upon another, hundreds deep, so that it was only at the edges that he could tell a single foot: as broad as a palm, without a claw. The freshest had broken the morning's frost, and their rims were still sharp. To one side there was a heap of bones, deer for the most part, cracked and grey. There was scat, old and new. Tufts of coarse hair had caught on the underside of the stone lip, at the height of a low grey back. And just within the mouth, where the daylight still reached, there was a place in the bare earth the length of a hound, scooped and worn as smooth as the seat of an old chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:181:"This morning," he said, very low. "Before we had laid the first cord. More than six. I should say eight, or nine; they have gone over each other's marks and I cannot do better." He pointed, without lifting his hand from his knee, at the newest prints. They led away from the mouth, down the slope, to the east. "Out. All of them, and none back since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:"Why here?" he said. "There's a thousand hollows in a country like this. There's a whole mountain two days west with caves in it. Why would a thing with teeth choose to lie under the one floor that the carters won't walk on?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:209:She stood a little apart, with her broad hands hanging open. She had told them at noon what they had in her, on this side of a stone, and she met their eyes one after another and did not pretend that it had altered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:221:It took him less time than it took Brom to shift the box higher on his shoulders and draw the strap tight. He had always written at the start of a thing. He did not know how to begin one without it, and he thought that this might be the last quiet minute of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:223:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:225:*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:229:There was a line that came next. He had written it at the foot of every entry since a shelf above a valley with no name, and for the last forty-three days it had ended in the same word. His pencil went to the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:3:Pike wrote the wall out again that evening, from the top, on a clean length of sailcloth, and Lira counted the lines as they went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:The room was warmer than Cael remembered it, and emptier. The cold term had sent half the crews home to their own fences, and the benches down both sides had room on them; but there was a fire in the great hearth that a man could have stood in, and it threw its light across the plaster where the old sheet had hung since the sixtieth day. A boy held the new cloth taut with both palms. The one hand moved over it, steadily, and went to the horn pot on the bench and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:She was not sitting. She stood in the middle of the floor with her arms folded and her weight on her own boot, and her lips moved at each name. The five of them had the boards nearest the door, where they had sat on the forty-ninth night, and on the boards between Brom's elbows lay a sheet of cream paper with a lamp beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:She said it quite quietly. Two lines that had stood over theirs since the day they came now stood under them, and the shingle pair were at the foot still. Cael looked for what the rung was made of, since he had learned in this room that it was always made of something. It was a mountain, less a cart. The wall had no place to say so. It said only that a hold which asked Pike who ought to walk a bad hill would now be given five names before it was given two others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:39:"The man at the top," he said. "You told us at the plank that he came in last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:[[TIER-B: Pike, in the board's room, of Teague — "Teague's nine up… He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice… I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. And the same man had stood at that plank a night ago, with a hundred marks under his hand, and had read it twice, and put it back under a stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:53:It had been the evening before, the eighty-ninth, with the five still a camp short of the river, when Teague counted his three off the ferry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:57:Three, and whole. That hip had had six days of road since a healer told him the price of it, and the bow had not once asked to shorten a stage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:59:He sent the boy to the smith's with the mules and went up to the plank by himself, as he always did, before he had so much as washed. Pike was taking in his book for the night. Teague read the oak from the left-hand post, a slip at a time, and at the second slip he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:77:He was given it, as he had been given a struck slip on the fifty-seventh day. It was good paper, heavier than anything the holds wrote on. He read it from the first word to the seal, and then he began again at the top, more slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred. That was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:83:The hirer was the broker, by the wax. Teague had carried that man's bales. He paid to the day, and argued every copper beforehand and none after; and he had never in his life sent a crew farther than his own landing. That was odd. It was not wrong. A broker posts for whoever puts money in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:101:They would be at the ferry tomorrow. He had kept a day ahead of them from Oxhollow without trying to, and he knew their pace, with a taped boy and a girl on one boot. The boy would read this plank as he had read a doorway, from one end to the other, leaving nothing out. He would find the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:"I shall be brief, since I am not disinterested, and I want that known before I begin." Her voice was small and exact. "I have three boxes on a cart outside. Every leaf in them is about the country we left, except the handful that I have written myself since a river. In ninety days I have met one thing on this side that somebody else thought worth a sheet of paper, and it is lying there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:121:"He says that there is a place on a road where an Arbiter stops. He says it stops at a line, and that a person knows the line by her own. If that is so, then it can be found with nothing but feet, by anybody, and found again in the same place the next day by somebody else. I have been taught that an Arbiter cannot be tested at all; that it is the one thing every instrument we own takes for granted. I should like to stand where one fails." She drew a breath. "I would go for nothing. I say so in order that nobody should count my hand as a vote for the hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:137:"I've walked the east road four times and I know every stone of it as far as that fence. Past the fence it's two days more, on ruts that the grain carts use, to a hill. Then it's whatever a surveyor leaves behind him, picked up, and brought back the same way." She looked round at them. "I'm saying it's a walk and a carry. I'm not saying it isn't the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:169:"And the sum. It is written in words, and then again, in a bracket. A person is taught to do that who has been warned that a figure may be altered and a word may not. I was so taught, at a ruled desk, when I was not a great deal older than he is." The mild eyes came up. "The place is given as a description. A day, and a rise, and a road that bends. Every carter in that meadow has a name for it, and our friend at the plank used the name at once. Whoever wrote this either did not know it or was not permitted to put it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:179:For some time the only sound was the fire. Cael looked at a cream sheet that had lain a month under a river stone, and saw it as he had not seen it at the plank: a thing with a ruled margin, lying on an inn's boards in a country that kept no paper. He had walked east for ninety days, and here was a piece of the west on his own table, with a seal on it that told him nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:193:"If we mark it in the morning, that is the ninety-first," she said. "The Oxhollow cart rests a day and goes east on the ninety-second, with our boxes, and we in front of it. That brings us to the fence on the afternoon of the ninety-third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:197:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:Brom was looking at his left wrist. Lira had her eyes on the great boot, and on what was taped inside it; she had asked for that ankle once, and been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed. Seln studied his cup. And Cael had stood by a well with a sentence ready, six days ago, and had found that he had no right to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:209:There were five. He drew the cream sheet toward him, and folded it once along its old fold, and put it inside Hesk's book for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:213:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:221:He gave the paper back to Pike in the morning, across the oak, before the mist was off the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:241:"So we could go four days and come home with a line and no money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:247:There was one thing more, and Cael had turned it over in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:249:"The two holds," he said to Pike. "With our names. We'll be nine days on this, if it goes as it's written. If we're not down in their fortnight, don't keep a bull waiting on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:251:"I'd not have. I told them a fortnight, and after that the top of the sheet." Pike blotted the strokes. "It's good of you to say it where he can hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:35:"So I'd heard, from a man who came in last night." Pike set his pen in its pot. "I'll say your line when I've read your paper. Read mine first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:37:Lira was already reading. She had gone to the left-hand post, from habit, and she was standing in front of two slips that had not been there on the seventieth morning. One was bark and one was a shingle, and on each of them, under a hold's mark and a line of words, stood a word in the square black hand, in capitals, which Cael had seen on this plank only once before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:41:It had had one name after it, that time. Under each of these there were five, set down in the order in which they had been spoken at the plank on the ninth day, and his own was the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:51:"Who they ought to have. One's a bull wants bringing down off the top grazing before the snow shuts it, and he's killed a dog. The other's a ford that wants staking. They'd each of them heard a line said in this meadow about a river and five dead hounds." He blotted something. "I told them you were six days off on a hill. They said they'd wait a fortnight. After that they'll have Teague."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:He had been written on a good many boards. A woman in Ardenmere had chalked him up with a question mark, and a panel had posted him at a rate that it had scattered on purpose, and for two years a house had put his name on a card every week because a schedule told it to. Nobody had ever before gone to the keeper of a book and asked who was good, and been given him. He had stood in an inn, forty days ago, and heard Pike say that he could not send a hold a crew that had never yet had anything go wrong. Something had gone wrong for them twice since then, and they had come back from it both times, and here it was on a shingle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:57:"A fortnight," said Lira. "They'd wait a fortnight, for us, with a bull loose." She looked round at the others, and her eyes were very bright. "He didn't say fast. Nobody said fast. They said *who*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:63:It had come down a mountain inside her shirt and six days along a valley, and it was soft at the folds. Pike laid it on the oak and put two fingers on it, and read it to himself from the careful writing at the head to the foot, where five strokes stood side by side over a horned head and three wavy lines; and then he read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:77:He stopped there, with his mouth a little open, as a man stops on a stair to feel for the next tread. Cael knew what the next one was, for he had heard it on a river bank at dusk, from a man with a band of leather on his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:105:He turned that over, since he had expected to. On the bank at Oxhollow he had written that he kept waiting to mind, and he had gone on waiting for six days of road. The line said that they had lost a cart, and it said so to thirty strangers, twice. And he stood under an eave with five bands of linen round his chest and felt only that a thing had been counted, by a person who counted everything alike; it was the seventh line under their names, and it was worth sixty-four, and both of those were so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:125:There was a curve of dust across the corner, and he looked at that for a breath before he understood it. The dust followed the rim of the stone exactly, a fine pale line, as a tide leaves its line on a beach; so the paper had not been slid there this morning. It had lain under that stone, in that one place, for long enough to take the stone's shape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:131:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:139:Cael had no other word for it. He had watched that face for eighty days, and it was never still; it looked at a thing and then looked again, and something in it went on working like a mill between the two looks. Now the one arm lay on the book and the brown eyes rested on a sheet of cream paper, and nothing in the face moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:151:He did not need to look, for he knew whose it was, and he knew what was folded in the middle box, forty yards off, with two lines ruled under two of its words. Karis had heard those two words once before, dropped by a woman with a sack of barley on her hip, and she had asked nobody what they meant for thirty-two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:"I have that noun," she said. Her voice was very careful. "I have had it since the fifty-eighth day, from a carter's wife, in three sentences, and I have had nobody to put a question to. You have just said it to me yourself, at your own plank. I think that I may ask now. What is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:165:Cael had heard *yours* and *everyone's*, and he had thought of a place behind his own breastbone, where a small bright thing had gathered once on a morning in a windowless room, and looked at him for eleven seconds, and gone out. It had been dark ever since, and he kept that thought where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:185:Cael knew the place. East of a square with a dot in it, two days of ticks out, a double line left its straight and went out to the left in a curve and came back to itself, as a path goes round a pond; and inside the curve a carter had drawn nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:197:Cael looked at the three things lying side by side on the tarred oak. There was a leaf in his own hand that held a stranger's passing word, and a calfskin that a carter had drawn for his wheels over twenty-two years, and a cream sheet under a seal that he had first seen burned into the end of a cask. None of them had been meant for the others' company. The woman with the barley had never looked at her husband's map with a pencil in her hand, and the carter had certainly never read a broker's paper; and yet all three were speaking of one patch of grass, two days beyond a fence, as though they had agreed on it beforehand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:201:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:205:"Nobody scratched. There were forty in this meadow that day, and when I'd done you could have heard the river. So I put the stone on it. A thing on a tack is a thing I'm offering."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:A hundred marks was more than everything that the board had paid them in ninety days, the mountain included. It was to walk two days east of a hold where they were known, and pick up something that somebody had left lying, and carry it home. He could find no opponent in it. There was a ring on a road, and a rule with no reason given, and instruments, which were only things; and he heard himself thinking that, and distrusted it, since he had thought the same about a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:223:"I'll not mark it tonight," said Pike. "That's not a thing I'd say of a fence. Take it in with you, and put it on your boards, and look at it with a lamp. If you bring it me in the morning I'll say it and write your strokes." He pushed the paper across the oak with one finger, until it lay in front of Cael. "And if you don't, it goes back where it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:229:He was at his door, as he had been on the seventy-first morning, and he looked past them at the place under his lean-to where a cart with a cut axle ought to have been standing. Brom told it again, and it was shorter this time, since a smith needed no telling what loose stone did to a wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:243:It was done. Cael had carried that six since the seventy-first morning, as one carries a stone in a boot, and he waited to feel lighter and did not. The smith had gone back into his shed already, and presently the rasp began again. A cart was only a cart, and the man had been paid for it twice over; all the same there would be a clean place under that lean-to for a good while, and Brom stood looking at it with his hands hanging until Karis took him by the sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:It was true, and he had not known it, and she had. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for ninety days, and thought himself a careful person; but a refusal that is made again at every stride is the opposite of a rule, for it is a door that somebody is holding shut with his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:273:He thought of a question that stood in his book under the fifty-ninth day, in her words, with nothing written beneath it. She had asked what she was becoming if nobody was measuring, and he had not answered, and nobody had. He did not suppose that a ring on a road would answer it either. But it would tell her one thing plainly, at one stride, without a sheet or a panel or a coach; and he saw that she had heard that in the old man's three short sentences before any of the rest of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:5:Cael stayed where he was, on the gravel at the water's edge, with the book shut on his knee and his left arm held in against five bands of linen. It was the start of something, and so he took the inventory, as he took it at the start of anything. Three ribs were broken on the left side, low; they had been taped on the eighty-first day, and the tape was to come off on the ninety-ninth and not before. The calf was bound in a healer's cloth and was closing by itself, as his hurts did. Lira had a shin that was four days old, and an ankle that no healer on this road was going to mend, and one boot. Brom's left wrist wore a linen cuff, and he was to hang nothing from it until the middle of the eighty-seventh day. Karis had been told to ask nothing of herself for three days more, and she was asleep against a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:7:There were three boxes, and they stood on the bare ground by a gate-house wall. That was the item he liked least. A hundred and sixteen marks lay in Karis's purse, and six of them were a smith's. The plank where those six would be paid was six days down a road, and each box weighed as much as a grown sheep. He had one man who was allowed to carry, and four who were not fit to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:17:"Where else is there grain? I'd meant him to go over the top, when the dry spell came, and be home in six days." The brown eyes came round. "You've told me what's over the top. So he goes round by the one-armed man's ferry, the same as his father went, and that's eight days out and eight home, and the frost won't wait on me while I think about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:21:"He's a lad and two mules. It's sixteen days of road, and I'd as soon he had somebody walking in front of him for the first six." The keeper tipped his stick at the gate-house wall. "Your boxes'd ride. There's room on the bed going out; it's coming home that she's full."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:27:So there it was. Cael went through it once, from habit, for the place where it cost something, and he could not find one. A column on a pasted leaf had ended in the wrong word, and the five of them had written it anyway, and for that a man was offering him wheels for six days. He had been paid for a great many things in his life, and most of them had been somebody's convenience. He did not think that he had ever before been done a kindness on the strength of a no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:33:He asked them in the yard, by the well, with the light gone grey. Brom looked at three boxes and then at his own left wrist, and Cael watched him weigh a road of six days against a rule of three, and put his hand up. Lira's was up already. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. They had to wake Karis for hers, and she raised it before her eyes were properly open and asked afterward what she had voted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:43:They lay that night in the keeper's cart-shed, under a heap of empty grain sacks that smelled of last year's barley, since their own blankets were at the bottom of a gorge. It was warmer than a bay of shale had been. He lay on his right side, where the ribs hung, and listened to four people breathing, and counted nine days to a fence in a valley where a healer meant to put her hands on him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:47:They went through the ford at first light on the eighty-fifth day, and nobody spoke until the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:49:The carter was a long young man with a narrow sleepy face and the largest feet that Cael had ever seen on anybody. He was the keeper's sister's son. He had two grey mules, and a cart a foot wider in the bed than the one that lay in the gorge. In the six days that followed he said perhaps forty words, and most of them were to the mules. The red oxen drew his wheels over for nothing, since the wheels were the hold's own. He walked through behind them with his breeches rolled to the thigh and his boots hung round his neck by their laces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:55:They were a day old. The dew had softened their edges, but the gravel gave way there to a strip of grey silt, and the silt had kept everything. Four people had come up out of the river in single file, each a crew's length behind the one in front, and two mules after them on one rein. He crouched, as far as five bands of linen would let him, and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:65:"I should think somebody does, about once a day." He stood up carefully. "They went up this bank at dusk, with the light going and a river to get over, and there isn't a scuffed print among the four of them. Nobody turned to look back. Nobody stepped out of the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:91:Brom made a sound into his sleeve. Lira looked at the boots, and at the road going away south between its alders, six days of it, and at her own bare foot with the linen crossed over it in a figure of eight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:109:By noon of the second day he hated the tape, as he had been told that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:111:He could have borne the pain of it; he had carried pain before, on worse terms, and priced it and gone on. What he could not bear was that the thing was always right. He would reach for the water-skin, or turn his head to see what Lira had laughed at, or simply draw the breath that a hill wanted; and five bands of linen would close across his side a hair before the bone did, and tell him *there*. Every time, when he stopped to check it, the tape had known and he had not. A person can forgive an instrument for being wrong. He was learning that it was a good deal harder to forgive one for being right four thousand times a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:115:A breath went in to a count of three before the tape spoke. On a level road he could take that breath at every fourth stride and never touch the edge. On a rise it wanted every third, and on the steepest of the valley's little hills every second, and there the breaths came so shallow and so quick that his head swam. He had always walked in fits. He had gone quick and then slow since he was a child on the lanes of Denvash, running to look at a thing and standing still to count it. He had watched a healer come down a mountain for a day and a half at one unbroken beat, and he had not been able to copy her for a hundred yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:117:He tried it now beside the near mule, since there was nothing else to do and six days in which to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:119:The mule did not hurry and did not stop. Its near fore came down, and he put his left foot down with it; four of those, and a breath, to the count of three; four more. Whenever he ran ahead of the beat the tape caught him, and whenever he fell behind it the cart's wheel came up at his heel. Between those two there was a place the width of a plank where neither of them touched him, and by the afternoon of the eighty-sixth day he could stay on the plank for a mile together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:121:It was a small thing to be proud of. He entered it all the same. In two years of other people's fights and eighty-six days of road, nobody had ever taught him anything that he had so little wished to learn, and it had been taught him by a strip of pitch and a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:125:He knew, since he could hear her. She had been told a month, and it was plain that she had taken the month as she had taken the mule, as a thing agreed to and not to be discussed. She set the great boot down on its whole sole at every step and looked at the country. There was a good deal of it to see. The road went south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, with the grey mountain shrinking behind their left shoulders, and twice a day it forded some small brown water where the mules drank below the crossing. She named nothing and timed nothing. Once, in the middle of the second afternoon, she said across the cart that she had never before walked anywhere slowly enough to see what was growing on a wall, and that it was mostly moss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:Karis slept. She woke to eat, and to ask which day it was and write the number down, and then she folded herself up again between two boxes with Seln's coat over her and was gone. Cael watched her do it and was easier each time. On the mountain she had looked at her own hands before she looked at anything else, and by the third morning on this road she had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:131:It was Brom that Cael watched on the eighty-seventh day, from the first light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:133:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:135:At the noon halt he cut a hazel rod from the roadside, a yard long and as straight as he could find, and pushed it upright into the turf of the halting-ground. Then he sat down on a chock-stone in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:139:"The middle of the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:141:"You can see the middle of the day. It's up there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:153:"Well?" said Lira. "Aren't you going to lift something? You've been looking at that cart for three days like a dog at a door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:165:The moss came on the fourth day, and the carter stopped his mules at the edge of it without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:189:"Walking," she said. "I'm allowed to walk. She said it'd walk in a week as well as the other, and that was eight days ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:207:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:213:"He's pleased about something," the carter said at last, to Cael. It was the longest speech of the six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:215:"He's been waiting eight mornings to pick that up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:223:They made their last camp on the evening of the eighty-ninth day, on a halting-ground at the top of a long hill, with a river shining below them in the dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:227:Karis was awake. She had been awake since noon, sitting up between the boxes with her notebook on her knee, and she had the colour back in her face that six days of sleep could give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:"Tomorrow is the ninetieth," she said. "The plank, and a smith. I have a slip in my coat with a horned head on it, and I want to hear what he does with a job that ended in a no." She turned a leaf. "And Thornwater on the ninety-third. That's four days, and two of them are road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:233:"She said nine days, and he said nine days back to her. I was there; I had my eyes shut, but I was there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:247:"Five halting-grounds, and not a new ash on one of them. The chock-stones are where they found them. They eat cold, and they sleep in a row with the mules at the windward end, and in the morning there's nothing to show for it but where the grass lay down." He turned a page back to look at what he had put. "We've had a fire every night since the ford, on the strength of a finding. He's had none for longer than I've known what a hold was, on the strength of never having needed one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:255:Cael had the day's lines to put down, and they were short ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:257:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:261:*A crew went down this road a day ahead of us and never left a mark out of its line. I read them at every halt. They'll have been at the plank tonight.*

