editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:151:"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:She had been taught to do it at fourteen, by attending to a place behind the breastbone, and it had been years since she had needed to do it on purpose; it was simply there when she wanted it, as a clerk is there behind a counter. It came now. It hung at the edge of her sight where it had always hung, small and steady and yellow-white, and it had about it the particular quality of attention that she had never been able to describe to anybody who did not have one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:95:"I heard." The purse came down over the cart's side in a small pale hand. "I shall want it understood that this is the board's money and it has bought a boot and a half's worth of leather that nobody can wear. In hand, a hundred and fourteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:257:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*

