editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:93:He stepped across, and for a breath he only stood, with his eyes half shut. Cael knew what he was reaching for, since he carried a piece of the same thing himself, sealed, and had felt it lift in him at every dusk for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:187:He held it out. She took it between both of hers, as she had taken a carter's palm on a plank at Thornwater, with two fingers of her right hand laid along the inside where the pulse was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:225:"Then the carters were right," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:261:The turf under his left boot was outside the line, and the turf under his right was within it, and he had the weight of both as he had the weight of his own hands. Nothing dimmed. He took another step, and a third, and the ground went on telling him what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:7:They took very little. Brom had the middle box on his back, with the chips in it and the paper, since whatever a surveyor had left would want carrying home in something. Karis had the long cord and the short one. There was the hedging bill, and Lira's knife, and Seln's, and a skin of water. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:53:"What is it?" said Brom. He had let the box down and was crouched beside it, with one big hand laid on the grey. "It's not slab. Slab's got a grain. It's not laid, either; I can't find a course."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:"None," she said. She was very pale. "It is not the colour of any of them, or the grain. It has no grain. I have every kind of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in that box, and a date for each, and this matches not one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:107:"I have been looking for what we were sent to fetch. A case, I supposed, or a tripod, or a chain. A peg. The mark where a peg had been." He turned one hand over. "There is nothing on this floor but ourselves, and nothing at the foot of any stone. I have walked a round in the grass at twenty yards from the lip, and another at forty. I find our own track, coming in from the west. I find nothing that a person has set down, or dropped, or driven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:"Why here?" he said. "There's a thousand hollows in a country like this. There's a whole mountain two days west with caves in it. Why would a thing with teeth choose to lie under the one floor that the carters won't walk on?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:231:He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:31:*Empty: sigil present, reserve gone.* She wrote it large, by feel. *Here: reserve present, sigil gone.* She turned the leaf. *These are not the same condition and must not be entered under one word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:133:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:161:They went round the north side with the ruts beside them. He could see where the carts had gone, forty yards off, keeping the same distance from the line the whole way, as exactly as if the carters had had a cord of their own; and he supposed that in a sense they had. A man who walked in front of a mule here for twenty-two years, with a Path in him, would have learned to the yard where he ceased to be comfortable.
