editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:He stood at the long house door with an empty bowl on the sill beside him. She had a round of seven gates, and she was late at every one of them already, and nobody had asked this of her; and she had just spent five days of it on a road that every carter she had ever mended told her to keep off. He had wanted to say the sentence for nine days. He was glad now, with his whole heart, that he had left it for her, since from him it would have been a request, and from her it was a decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:251:It was no bigger than a grain of barley; it caught, and the flame came up round it, ordinary and smoking, and the seed was gone into it, and Karis let out her breath in something that was not quite a laugh. He had last seen her do that across sixty yards of mountain, with her hands shaking like a rope under load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred. That was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:137:"I've walked the east road four times and I know every stone of it as far as that fence. Past the fence it's two days more, on ruts that the grain carts use, to a hill. Then it's whatever a surveyor leaves behind him, picked up, and brought back the same way." She looked round at them. "I'm saying it's a walk and a carry. I'm not saying it isn't the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:161:"I have listened to four cases that turn upon what this says," he said. "I should like to add what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:165:"There is a margin. It has been pricked, here and here, at even distances, with a wheel; that is done so that every sheet from a desk is ruled alike. No hold has such a wheel. The broker's clerk writes on whatever comes to hand." His finger moved to the head of the sheet. "*Recovery.* One word, set by itself, with a stop after it. That is how a class of business is named at the top of a paper that will afterward be put away with others of its class. A carter would have begun, *I have lost.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:169:"And the sum. It is written in words, and then again, in a bracket. A person is taught to do that who has been warned that a figure may be altered and a word may not. I was so taught, at a ruled desk, when I was not a great deal older than he is." The mild eyes came up. "The place is given as a description. A day, and a rise, and a road that bends. Every carter in that meadow has a name for it, and our friend at the plank used the name at once. Whoever wrote this either did not know it or was not permitted to put it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:227:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five. The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:"I am told so." The clerk looked at the five as if they had been bales of a kind that he had not handled before. "My master had it by the last sack, sealed, with the sum counted in a bag and a line of direction. He was to post it and to lodge the price. Whatever is brought down, I am to see it at the landing and put our mark to the slip, and it goes west by the next boat." He swallowed. "I don't know what the things are. I was not told who wants them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:21:They went over with the Oxhollow cart, a copper a wheel and a copper a beast out of the carter's own bag. Halfway across Brom lifted his head. In the round shed on the eastern bank something brown was walking its circle, steadily, with one white foot coming round and round into the light, and Brom watched that foot for the rest of the crossing with the face of a man who has seen a child of his doing well at a trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:73:Pike nodded slowly. He opened the book at the leaf with five names, and set the flat stone on its upper corner, and turned his shoulder to the meadow. There were perhaps thirty people within hearing, and Cael saw the hide-woman among them, with her knife on her thigh, and under the ash an old carter who lifted his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:81:It went down the meadow in that level carrying voice, past five fires. A woman looked at Lira's boot. A man by the nearest tent said something to his neighbour, low, and the neighbour looked at the strange cart, and then at the empty place beside the smith's shed where a hired one had stood. The Oxhollow carter had halted his mules at the edge of the grass to listen. At *carts go round* he had nodded slowly, like a person who hears his own errand read out to strangers; and at *cart lost* he looked at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:"I have that noun," she said. Her voice was very careful. "I have had it since the fifty-eighth day, from a carter's wife, in three sentences, and I have had nobody to put a question to. You have just said it to me yourself, at your own plank. I think that I may ask now. What is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:157:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"That's what a carter knows. That's what I know. There's not a man on the ferry knows a word more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:185:Cael knew the place. East of a square with a dot in it, two days of ticks out, a double line left its straight and went out to the left in a curve and came back to itself, as a path goes round a pond; and inside the curve a carter had drawn nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:197:Cael looked at the three things lying side by side on the tarred oak. There was a leaf in his own hand that held a stranger's passing word, and a calfskin that a carter had drawn for his wheels over twenty-two years, and a cream sheet under a seal that he had first seen burned into the end of a cask. None of them had been meant for the others' company. The woman with the barley had never looked at her husband's map with a pencil in her hand, and the carter had certainly never read a broker's paper; and yet all three were speaking of one patch of grass, two days beyond a fence, as though they had agreed on it beforehand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:201:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:213:"What's a surveyor want out there?" said Brom. "On ground a carter won't put a wheel on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:The middle box stood open on the barn floor, and she had been at the others since before he woke. She had the lid off the third. He came up the ladder in time to see her lifting out the masonry chips, each in its twist of paper with a number inked on the twist, and laying them in a row on a sack; and when the last was out there was a flat canvas bag at the bottom of the box, sewn shut and sealed, with five thumb-marks in the wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:29:Lira had a boot in each hand. One was the carter's left, which she had found standing outside the barn door at first light. She hung it on the mule's pack by its laces, with great care, against the other one going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:117:They walked it quiet. That was the hold, as it was on any road, and here it needed no telling. Seln went fifty paces ahead and never once looked back. The ruts were plain enough; the grain carts had used them within the month, and here and there he found the mark of a small neat hoof, and beside it, on the low side, a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:119:He watched the healer walk, since it had become a habit. She kept the carter's pace. She was below the mule on every slope, though the slopes were so gentle here that he could not always have said which side was the low one, and she changed over without seeming to think. He set himself to her beat, and breathed to the tape, and for long stretches of that afternoon the tape had nothing to say to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:143:"What do the carters say?" she asked. "I had it from you at the long house door, in a sentence. I should like it as they give it, if you'll tell me. I shan't ask twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:145:"They say a healer's no good there. That's the words. *You'll not want her in the ring; she's no good there.* I had it first from a carter at the first gate I ever mended at, and I took it that he meant I'd be frightened." Oryn laced the boot. "Then I had it from six more, in the same words, and not one of them a man to be frightened of a field. So it's a thing that was seen once, by somebody, and handed down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:171:Cael looked at the ground between the turn of the road and the stones, and it told him nothing. There was no ditch, and no bank, and no change in the grass. A carter of twenty-two years had drawn that curve on a calfskin and put nothing inside it, and Cael could not have said, standing where the man must have stood, how the man had known where to bend his wheels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:13:"I've a cart goes out at first light," he said. "For grain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:17:"Where else is there grain? I'd meant him to go over the top, when the dry spell came, and be home in six days." The brown eyes came round. "You've told me what's over the top. So he goes round by the one-armed man's ferry, the same as his father went, and that's eight days out and eight home, and the frost won't wait on me while I think about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:19:Cael said nothing. He had learned on a hill to let a carter come to the end of his own load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:43:They lay that night in the keeper's cart-shed, under a heap of empty grain sacks that smelled of last year's barley, since their own blankets were at the bottom of a gorge. It was warmer than a bay of shale had been. He lay on his right side, where the ribs hung, and listened to four people breathing, and counted nine days to a fence in a valley where a healer meant to put her hands on him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:49:The carter was a long young man with a narrow sleepy face and the largest feet that Cael had ever seen on anybody. He was the keeper's sister's son. He had two grey mules, and a cart a foot wider in the bed than the one that lay in the gorge. In the six days that followed he said perhaps forty words, and most of them were to the mules. The red oxen drew his wheels over for nothing, since the wheels were the hold's own. He walked through behind them with his breeches rolled to the thigh and his boots hung round his neck by their laces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:69:The carter had halted his mules on the bank and was waiting, with his sleepy face turned toward them. He had seen Lira's foot. He looked at it for some time, and then he went round to the cart's tail and took down a pair of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:73:"Two marks," said the carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:87:The carter thought about it with real care, and the mules stood, and the river went past.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:105:"Yes. That's her tape. I'd know where it was in a barrel." She tied the left boot to the cart's tail by its laces, where it hung all the way to Lowmarch, turning slowly, like a sign over a shop. "Don't anybody tell me what it looks like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:Karis slept. She woke to eat, and to ask which day it was and write the number down, and then she folded herself up again between two boxes with Seln's coat over her and was gone. Cael watched her do it and was easier each time. On the mountain she had looked at her own hands before she looked at anything else, and by the third morning on this road she had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:133:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:145:So they ate, and the shadow drew in. Nobody spoke of it, but Cael noticed that nobody strayed far either. The carter chewed, and looked from the rod to the big man and back, with the mild puzzlement of a person watching the customs of a foreign hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:165:The moss came on the fourth day, and the carter stopped his mules at the edge of it without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:171:The carter lifted one shoulder. "My father's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:179:"One in ten," he said. "Maybe one in six. They've gone soft from the middle, where you can't see it, and they'll bear a man. I'd not put a loaded axle on them." He looked back at the carter. "What did you weigh, coming this way last time?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:195:She went out along the logs. She did not run them, and Cael, who had watched her run sixty-two hundreds of a mountain, found it stranger to see her walk these. She set the packed boot down in the middle of each log and leaned on it, and waited, with her arms a little out from her sides; and at every one that gave she said "Soft," without turning her head, and stepped over. Seln followed three logs behind with a lump of chalk that the carter kept for his tallies, and drew a cross.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:199:Then they emptied the cart. The mules were led over one at a time, picking their way between the chalk marks with their ears laid back. After them the cart itself went across with her bed bare, the carter at her head, and Seln and Cael each at a wheel to turn it by the spokes whenever the rim came up to a cross. He could do that much. It was done at the height of his hip and it asked nothing of his ribs that the tape would not allow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:211:He carried it the whole way over. He came back for the left-hand one, and then for the right; and when Seln stepped forward for the third, since three crossings of that moss was a long way under a box, Brom shook his head and took that too. The carter watched him go by each time with a hundredweight in his arms and a look of deep content on his plain face, and plainly could not make out what he was seeing. Carrying boxes was only what a big man was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:213:"He's pleased about something," the carter said at last, to Cael. It was the longest speech of the six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:225:It was Lowmarch's river. He knew its colour, and the way it bent. They had come back to it from the west, by a bank that he had never stood on; and somewhere down there in the dark was a round thatched shed with a drum in it, and a horse walking a circle that had once drawn their cart across a province. Seln had a fire going with his flint. The carter sat at it as a matter of course, and held his enormous feet to the warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:253:Brom had the carter's pot between his knees, since their own kettle was at the bottom of a gorge, and he was scouring it with a handful of grass, slowly, with his lips moving. Cael knew that look as well. He was getting two speeches ready, one for a ferryman and one for a smith, and he was putting the worse of them first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:257:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*

